+//! makes the search fast. A byte the needle does not hold moves the window
+//! by the needle's whole length.
+//!
+//! The jump is computed from the needle's bytes *except its last one*, and
+//! that exclusion is the whole of the safety argument. The window's last
+//! byte is compared before the jump is computed, so a window that ends in
+//! the needle's own last byte is never stepped over: that byte is not in
+//! the table, and a byte that is not in the table moves the window by the
+//! needle's whole length, which lands on the next window rather than past
+//! it. The backwards search is the same search with the two ends swapped --
+//! the window is aligned by its first byte and the table is built over the
+//! needle's bytes except its first one.
